<h1>Import and export</h1>
<p>Every import has the same layout: the source in a large editor, the ways in on top (<i>Paste</i>, <i>From file…</i>, <i>From URL</i>, <i>Example</i>), what will be created on the right, and the action in the footer. A parse error is marked in the text as well as said under the editor.</p>
<p class="doc-lead">Bring requests in from a spec or a command; take them out as text.</p>
<ul>
  <li><b>OpenAPI 3 (JSON)</b> — the spec is in a code editor; paste it, read it from a file or a URL, or start from the example. The operations appear on the right grouped by tag: tick what you want, filter, then <i>Import</i>.
    The server becomes <code>&#123;&#123;baseUrl&#125;&#125;</code> and the security scheme the auth of the folder. YAML specs are not read yet.</li>
  <li><b>cURL</b> — paste a command to get a request; or copy the open request as cURL, with variables filled in or kept as placeholders.</li>
  <li><b>.http / .rest</b> (IntelliJ HTTP Client) — import a file, with the environments of its <code>http-client.env.json</code>; export a collection back. JavaScript response handlers are read and reported, never run.</li>
  <li><b>WSDL / SOAP envelope</b> — see <i>Event streams, cookies and SOAP</i>.</li>
  <li><b>Generate code</b> — the open request as code in a few languages.</li>
</ul>
